{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"limitless-taurine-egret","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Outbound parent-set can never be carried by a reconcile pass on Data Center (two gates that do not compose)\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound while building story 5200's missing row-12-OUTBOUND live cell, by an agent instructed to establish\nthe real contract before writing an oracle rather than assume a round-trip was supported. It is a PRODUCT\ngap, not a test gap.\n\n## Mechanism — each gate is defensible alone; on DC they are DISJOINT\n\n**The APPLY side wants a SUB-TASK child.** `adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` `set_parent` (:668-712)\nreads the issue, checks `fields.issuetype.subtask` (:693-694), and for a sub-task writes\n`issue.update(fields={\"parent\": {\"key\": parent_key}})` (:711-712). For a NON-sub-task child it raises\n`NotImplementedError` (:695-710) naming the reason: epic membership on DC is the \"Epic Link\" CUSTOM FIELD\nwritten through the Agile API under `greenhopper`, not `fields.parent`. That decline is deliberate and\ncorrect — it stops `fields.parent` being written and silently no-op'd.\n\n**The EMIT side wants an EPIC parent.** `outbound_field_diff._resolve_local_parent:136-139` returns\n`(False, None)` — omitting the parent field ENTIRELY — for any parent whose local `ticket_type` is not\n`epic`. That is bug 8b25's hierarchy guard, built over `local_ticket_types`\n(`outbound_differ.py:475-477`) and already unit-covered by\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/conflict/test_parent_hierarchy_guard.py`.\n\n**A DC sub-task's parent is a STANDARD issue, which imports as local `ticket_type` `\"task\"`.** So the one\nchild shape DC accepts (sub-task) can only have a parent shape the emit gate refuses (non-epic). The sets\nare disjoint: **no reconcile pass can ever emit an outbound parent-set that DC would accept.**\n\nBoth gates were written for Cloud's next-gen Epic-parent hierarchy, where they compose fine. Nobody\ncomposed them against DC's model.\n\n## Why this is the FIFTH instance of a known class\n\n\"Cloud has the translation, DC never got its half\" — d067 (outbound status submitted as a field edit,\nnever a transition), 8d68 (inbound UPDATE never minted an identity), 751e (empty assignee never\nunassigns), 2b16 / 88d9 (inbound link removal and parent clear never emitted). Every one was a SILENT\nsuccess: no traceback, no alert, the pass reports OK. This is the same shape — a local parent/child\nrelationship simply never reaches Data Center, and nothing reports that it did not.\n\n`dispatch_one.py:571-578` swallows `set_parent`'s exception (warn-and-continue, keyed at :546), so even\nthe DECLINE is invisible; but on DC the decline is not even reached, because the emit gate dropped the\nfield first.\n\n## Scope note — deliberately NOT gating e369\n\nRecorded rather than folded into epic e369, consistent with the operator's ruling on\n`e39f-5055-f5af-424a` (a DC write-path gap ruled follow-up, not gating). Two supporting reasons:\n * the fix is a HIERARCHY-MODEL change, not a patch — either the emit gate learns DC's sub-task shape or\n   DC's `set_parent` learns the Epic Link field. Either is a design pass with a real blast radius.\n * closing e369 does not assert this works. Its operator-attested criterion is bidirectional reconcile\n   (an issue created in DC appears locally; a local EDIT appears in DC), both proven live; no criterion\n   claims outbound parent sync.\n\n**I am flagging this as a BORDERLINE call.** The operator's earlier ruling on the three J11-surfaced\ndefects took the strictest available option, and unlike e39f this one does NOT fail safe — it is a silent\nDIVERGENCE, not a safe refusal. If the operator prefers the strict reading, reparenting this under e369\nis one command. The tension is recorded here on purpose rather than resolved quietly.\n\n## What story 5200 does and does not now cover\n\n5200's row-12-outbound cell (change 1283) asserts the PRIMITIVE at the transport level —\n`dc_transport.set_parent(child, B)` then a raw-REST read-back of `fields.parent` — following the same\nprecedent row 14 outbound set for the same reason. It deliberately does NOT assert the pass-level\nsuppression as correct behaviour, because that would freeze this gap into the suite.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a test asserts, at the pass level, that a local parent/child relationship syncable on Cloud is\n      ALSO emitted for DC — i.e. the emit gate and DC's accepted child shape overlap for at least one\n      real configuration\n- [ ] the chosen fix is stated explicitly: either the emit gate learns DC's sub-task shape, or DC's\n      `set_parent` writes the Epic Link custom field through the Agile API — with the rejected option and\n      the reason recorded\n- [ ] the 8b25 hierarchy guard's Cloud behaviour is UNCHANGED, pinned by its existing tests in\n      `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/conflict/test_parent_hierarchy_guard.py`\n- [ ] a parent that genuinely cannot be represented on the deployment surfaces an OPERATOR-VISIBLE signal\n      rather than being silently dropped — the swallow at `dispatch_one.py:571-578` is why this class\n      stayed invisible for five instances running\n- [ ] mutation-checked, with verdicts recorded here","id":"39c1-2a32-b564-4b4b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Outbound parent-set can never be emitted for DC: the epic-parent emit gate and DC's sub-task-child apply gate are disjoint"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785730356429557001,"uuid":"f15f5e22-2d99-4544-b6cd-f7462ce1c6fe"}